statement:
Let $p$ be a prime and let $\Lambda$ be a nonempty directed partially ordered set (every pair of indices has a common upper bound). For each $\lambda\in\Lambda$, let $I_\lambda$ and $C_\lambda$ be finite groups, let $P_\lambda$ be a normal subgroup of $I_\lambda$ whose order is a power of $p$, and let $	heta_\lambda:I_\lambda	o C_\lambda$ be a surjective homomorphism with kernel $P_\lambda$. For $\mu\ge\lambda$, suppose given surjective homomorphisms $u_{\mu\lambda}:I_\mu	o I_\lambda$ and $v_{\mu\lambda}:C_\mu	o C_\lambda$ such that $u_{\mu\lambda}(P_\mu)=P_\lambda$ and $	heta_\lambda u_{\mu\lambda}=v_{\mu\lambda}	heta_\mu$. Assume the identity and composition laws $u_{\lambda\lambda}=\mathrm{id}$, $u_{
u\lambda}=u_{\mu\lambda}u_{
u\mu}$ for $
u\ge\mu\ge\lambda$, and the corresponding laws for $v$. Give the finite groups their discrete topologies. Define $I=\varprojlim_\lambda I_\lambda$ and $C=\varprojlim_\lambda C_\lambda$ as the groups of compatible families in the respective products (the inverse-limit convention of [lem-galois-inverse-limit-surjective]), with their subspace topologies, and put $P=\{(x_\lambda)\in I:x_\lambda\in P_\lambda	ext{ for every }\lambda\}=\varprojlim_\lambda P_\lambda$, with its subspace topology. Then $	heta((x_\lambda)_\lambda)=(	heta_\lambda(x_\lambda))_\lambda$ is a continuous surjective homomorphism $I	o C$ whose kernel is the closed normal subgroup $P$, and the induced map $I/P	o C$, with the quotient topology on $I/P$, is an isomorphism of topological groups. Every finite continuous quotient of $P$ (the image of a continuous surjective homomorphism to a finite discrete group) has order a power of $p$.

